{
  "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
  "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:25 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Christopher J. Paolella",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PAOLELLA: The proviso doesn't say any employee of the United States who is authorized to carry out a search, seizure, or arrest. It used the term \"any officer of the United States.\" And I believe that the term \"officer\" carries some water here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (government)",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Pooler, the Government has not taken the view that the Third Circuit was correct and Pooler, as far as I can tell, except within the Third Circuit, is binding precedent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "How long ago was it that the United States took the opposite position, the position argued by amicus here?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (government)",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Government took a position that never was a position that made it to the Solicitor General's office."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (government)",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "those torts serve as a rough approximation of what Congress anticipated would be the areas where it thought the United States should be liable, when we are talking about Federal law enforcement officers."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yang, is what's the difference between those two things?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Would an officer of the EPA be a law enforcement officer when he writes a letter to a company saying, you know, you are violating section such-and-such of the statute? Is that a law enforcement officer?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BUCHOLTZ: Well, the person who writes the letter may qualify as a law enforcement officer under the definition, but that's a different question, I would submit, than whether that -- whether that act constitutes law enforcement activity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Government would say that because OSHA isn't a criminal law in the traditional sense and an OSHA inspector or an OSHA agent isn't enforcing criminal law in the colloquial sense, that that shouldn't be covered."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: So how does that argument help your case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "which is all of this depends on how broadly or narrowly we define law enforcement activities."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0002-0012",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Most Federal courts have adopted a broad interpretation of the law enforcement proviso. So if adopting a broader interpretation here would open the flood gates, the flood gates are already open, and they have been for 40 years in most of the country. And we haven't seen a flood of FTCA suits brought by prisoners, especially since the passage of the PLRA --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: You -- you support the government's position on who's an officer, right? So it wouldn't include EPA. Is that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Paolella",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. PAOLELLA: I -- I don't think that the issue has really been litigated in the Federal courts. It simply hasn't come up. And I think that that's an indication that it's -- it's a workable test. It's -- it's not something -- that there are many, many cases, the vast majority of cases, involve individuals who will be by any reasonable definition core law enforcement officers. As far as I am aware, every Federal court to address the issue has defined correctional officers as law enforcement officers. You know -- as officers, as that term is used."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Paolella",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And I think it's important to keep in mind that the best evidence of Congress's intent is the text of the statute. And the fundamental problem with amicus's position is that he very ably uses tools for construing ambiguous statutes to construe a statute that at its core is not ambiguous, it's precise and it's definite."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Bucholtz, this Court appointed you as an amicus curiae to brief and argue the case in support of the judgment below, and you have ably discharged that responsibility for which the Court is grateful."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Paolella",
      "case_id": "2012-11-10362",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "89fe56b7fcc8aa56d5ba3f914638ed044ec6296aecc3302261620039df0ff237",
      "evidence_id": "ev-89fe56b7fcc8-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "result, we would urge the Court to reverse."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
